data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In EF Core 9, the Cosmos DB provider **removed synchronous I/O by default** — sync methods like `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, and `SaveChanges()` throw `InvalidOperationException`. In .NET 10 you could opt back in via a compatibility flag to keep sync-over-async working.

**In .NET 11 (EF Core 11), that opt-in is removed entirely.** There is no longer any way to use synchronous LINQ/SaveChanges with the Cosmos provider. Every call must be async.

### What you need to change

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

### Key points

- **All callers** must propagate `async/await` up the call chain (controllers, services, etc.).
- `Add()` stays synchronous — it only tracks the entity in memory. The I/O happens in `SaveChangesAsync()`.
- Remove any `CosmosDbContextOptionsBuilder.AllowSynchronousIO` or equivalent opt-in flag from your `DbContext` configuration — it will cause a build error or be silently ignored.

The underlying reason: the Cosmos DB SDK is **async-only by design**. The old sync path was always just `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` under the hood, which risks deadlocks and thread-pool starvation.
